\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
Korvane Cold Chain Sp. z o.o. \u183\'b7 SOP KC-OPS-SOP-031 \u183\'b7 revision 6 \u183\'b7 effective 2 September 2024 \u183\'b7 replaces rev. 5 of 17 March 2023}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
Owner: Operations (J. Lindqvist). Written by Wac\u322\'3faw Bielicki, senior shift supervisor at GDY-1, because Jarek asked me nicely and then less nicely. Valid at all five depots. Printed copies are "uncontrolled", I know nobody reads the footer, but there it is. Any laminated sheet at any depot that disagrees with this text is wrong, Hamburg, and yes I mean the one next to the coffee machine.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af11\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf18\f6\fs26\b\dbch\af10{\loch
0. Why this exists}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
I started on the quay at Gdynia in 2004, back when a reefer was a box with a unit hanging off the nose and a clipboard hanging off the door, and in twenty years I have watched exactly one kind of mistake destroy more cargo than all the others together: somebody was in a hurry at the beginning. Cargo does not go bad on the vessel. It goes bad on the dock, in the first forty minutes, and you only find out in Rotterdam on a Thursday. So this SOP is mostly about the beginning, and a little bit about what to do when you got it wrong anyway, which is the part the auditors like.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
Rev. 6 changes three things against rev. 5. The pre-cool for chilled loads was 3 hours and is now 4.5 hours. The loading window for chilled boxes was 45 minutes and is now 38. And the excursion ladder grew a fourth rung, because of what happened on 14 June (you know which one). Everything else stays as it was, so please stop asking me in the canteen.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af11\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf18\f6\fs26\b\dbch\af10{\loch
1. Pre-cooling the empty box}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
Nobody loads into a warm box. "Warm" does not mean what the display says. A unit that was plugged in twenty minutes ago can show +4 on the screen while the floor and the steel walls are still at yard temperature, and they will bleed heat into your pallets for the first hour of the voyage. The clock for pre-cooling starts when Tidewalk shows the first "setpoint reached" message for that box, not when you plugged it in, and not when the driver tells you it feels cold.}
