\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
If the yard is warmer than 28 \u176\'b0C when you plug in, add 90 minutes to every line of that table, no negotiation. We had a very clever young man at Waltershof in 2019 who argued that fruit "doesn't care". The fruit cared. Also look at the drain plugs and the T-floor before you start the clock: a box with standing water or a dead cardboard layer from the last customer is not a box, it is a problem you have decided to pay for later.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch\b\loch
Product temperature at the dock.}{\loch
 Before the first pallet goes in, somebody takes a core reading of that pallet with the hand probe. Chilled goods go in at +6.0 \u176\'b0C or colder. Frozen goods go in at \u8722\'3f15.0 \u176\'b0C or colder. Between \u8722\'3f15.0 and \u8722\'3f12.0 \u176\'b0C frozen product may be loaded only with a signed waiver from the customer's representative standing in front of you (a forwarded email from "someone in Sales" is not a waiver). Warmer than \u8722\'3f12.0 \u176\'b0C, you refuse it, take photographs, and ring Duty Ops. I do not care how long the lorry has been waiting.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af11\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\keep\widctlpar\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf18\f6\fs26\b\dbch\af10{\loch
2. Building the load}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
The red load line is painted on the inside wall and it is a legal limit for the box. Ours is stricter. Maximum pallet height, pallet plus cargo, is 2.18 m in a 40' high-cube and 2.06 m in a 20' or a standard-height 40'. That leaves you at least 12 cm of air above the top layer for the return air to travel, and if you stack to the red line "because it fits" you will hear from me personally.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af4\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hyphpar0\cf0\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af9\langfe1033{\loch
Number of pallets: at most 23 euro-pallets in a 40', at most 10 in a 20'. Pharma loads are limited to 20 in a 40', because the gaps matter more than the revenue. No pallet heavier than 1,180 kg gross, whatever the label says. Leave 8 cm between the last pallet and the doors, 4 cm to each side wall, and keep the first 20 cm in front of the unit bulkhead completely clear. Air has to go somewhere. Cartons wedged against the bulkhead are the single most common reason a perfectly healthy unit "doesn't hold temperature" in the report.}
